++ dedupe-board --board-dir ~/myproject --apply    # collapse the groups
++ ```
++ 
+++**Recycled-id repair (`--by-id`)**: live boards also carry many DISTINCT
+++findings filed under ONE recycled task id (e.g. six `QA-ASCE-1` rows, each a
+++different finding: header-frozen, spawn-pool exhausted, port-range exhausted
+++x2, bunker-dropped). The fingerprint mode correctly refuses to collapse them
+++(their fingerprints differ). Use `--by-id` to repair this hygiene:
+++
+++```bash
+++dedupe-board --board-dir ~/asce --by-id              # dry-run: show groups
+++dedupe-board --board-dir ~/asce --by-id --apply      # close later lines
+++dedupe-board --board-dir ~/asce --by-id --json       # machine-readable report
+++```
+++
+++`--by-id` keeps the EARLIEST line per id, closes each later line as
+++`superseded-by-earliest` (status=complete, worker_summary naming the kept
+++line), and preserves the closed line's original title+reasoning in an audit
+++event. Already-terminal later lines (complete/failed) are left untouched.
+++`--json` emits the report as JSON (only supported with `--by-id`).
+++
+++**Fresh-id rule**: a lane must ALWAYS mint a fresh task id for a new finding.
+++An id-recycled filing — a new finding under an old key — is never a
+++re-observation of the same task; `--by-id` repairs boards where lanes re-used
+++a cycle number instead of advancing the id.
+++
++ `import --renumber` (BT-022) applies the same "never lose a finding" idea to
++ round-tripped exports: when an imported row shares an id with an existing row
++ but its content differs, plain `import` skips it, while
